Private key

64 bytes, two independent 32-byte keys concatenated.

    offset  size  field
    0       32    X25519 private key
    32      32    Ed25519 private key

The X25519 half is a raw scalar. Identity stores the bytes exactly as
supplied and returns them unchanged; clamping happens inside the
curve implementation when the scalar is used.

    load_private_key   stores prv_bytes verbatim
                       RNS/Identity.py#prv_bytes
    get_private_key    returns them verbatim
                       RNS/Identity.py#get_private_key
    curve25519 clamps  at exchange and base mult
                       RNS/Cryptography/X25519.py#_fix_secret

Two consequences, both recorded as vectors in test/keyset/unclamped and
test/keyset/clamped:

    An unclamped scalar round-trips through Identity unchanged. An
    implementation that clamps on load will export a different private
    key than it imported.

    The public key, and therefore the identity hash, is derived after
    clamping. An unclamped scalar and its clamped form yield the same
    public key and the same identity hash.

The Ed25519 half is a seed in the sense of RFC 8032, not an expanded
secret scalar.

The two halves are unrelated. Neither is derived from the other, and
there is no master seed.

    RNS/Identity.py#prv_bytes
    RNS/Cryptography/Ed25519.py#Ed25519PrivateKey
    RNS/Cryptography/X25519.py#_fix_secret


Public key

64 bytes, in the same order.

    offset  size  field
    0       32    X25519 public key
    32      32    Ed25519 public key

    RNS/Identity.py#pub_bytes


Identity hash

    identity_hash = SHA-256(public_key)[0:16]

16 bytes. Derived from the public key only. The private key does not
enter the derivation.

    RNS/Identity.py#update_hashes


Signature

Ed25519 over the message bytes, 64 bytes, as specified in RFC 8032.
Signing is deterministic: the same key and message always produce the
same signature.

Identity.sign applies no prefix, no domain separator and no prior
hashing. The message is passed to Ed25519 as it stands. This is what
test/signature/ pins; Ed25519 itself has published vectors and is not
restated here.

    RNS/Identity.py#sign


Canonical signatures, and the backend in the pin

A signature is R || S, and RFC 8032 section 5.1.7 admits only S < L,
where L is the group order. Adding L to S gives a second encoding of
the same signature: R is untouched, and an implementation that reduces
S rather than range-checking it accepts both.

python-rns ships two curve implementations and picks one at import
time. They disagree here.

    openssl backend     rejects S + L      the pin
    internal fallback   accepts S + L      RNS/Cryptography/pure25519

    RNS/Cryptography/Provider.py#PROVIDER_PYCA

So the verdict on this input is decided by what is installed, not by
the version. That is why every meta file names the backend as well as
the commit, and why tools/gen refuses to run under the other one.

test/signature/non-canonical-s and test/announce/non-canonical-s carry
the two forms. The announce decodes cleanly and its destination hash
matches; only signature_valid is no, and it is no because of the pin.

cmd/dump range-checks S itself, because tweetnacl does not. Without
that check it reports a valid announce for a packet the pinned backend
drops, which would make it the second implementation of something other
than the reference. The check is in cmd/dump.c, not in the vendored
file, which is not edited.

Two consequences for an implementation:

    A verifier that reduces S instead of checking it will accept
    announces python-rns 1.4.2 drops. It interoperates in one
    direction only.

    A signer never produces S >= L, so this costs nothing to get
    right on the sending side. It is a receiving-side rule.


Vector format: identity

kind: identity, determinism: encode

raw is one line:

    line 1    public key, 64 bytes

expect names the public key, its two halves, and the identity hash.

raw carries the public key rather than the private key because the
Reticulum-specific facts are the concatenation order and the hash.
Deriving a public key from a private one is X25519 and Ed25519, which
are standard primitives with their own published vectors.


Vector format: keyset

kind: keyset, determinism: encode

raw is one line:

    line 1    private key, 64 bytes

expect adds the derived public key, its two halves, and the identity
hash. These vectors do cross the boundary into the curve primitives,
because the derivation is where the two halves of a Reticulum private
key are told apart: the first is an X25519 scalar and the second an
Ed25519 seed, and swapping them produces a key that still looks
well-formed.

test/keyset/unclamped and test/keyset/clamped carry the clamping pair. As
identity vectors the two would be byte-identical, which is precisely
the property they exist to record.


Vector format: signature

kind: signature, determinism: decode

raw is three lines:

    line 1    public key, 64 bytes, in Reticulum form
    line 2    the message
    line 3    the signature, 64 bytes

expect names the Ed25519 half of the public key, the message length,
the SHA-256 of the message, the signature, and the verdict.

Decode class: expect records the message by length and digest rather
than by content, so raw cannot be rebuilt from it.


Vector format: sign

kind: sign, determinism: decode

raw is two lines:

    line 1    private key, 64 bytes
    line 2    the message

expect names the private key and its Ed25519 half, the public key
derived from it, the message length and digest, and the signature.

The signature is not an input here. test/signature hands a decoder a
signature and asks whether it verifies; this one hands it a key and a
message and asks what it produces. Ed25519 signing is deterministic,
RFC 8032 section 5.1.6: the nonce comes from the key and the message
and not from a random source, so the answer is a single value.

An implementation that draws a random nonce, or signs with the X25519
half of the Reticulum private key, verifies every signature the
reference makes and passes every other vector here. What it emits is an
announce and a link proof that no reference accepts.

The value is the one upstream asserts at
tests/identity.py#sig_from_key_0, which is an assertion about
signing. test/signature adopted the same value for the other direction.

These two kinds are the only decode-class vectors in the corpus that
decoded. Every other kind is encode class, and cmd/check rebuilds raw
from expect on every run for every vector that decoded.

The message is given by its bytes rather than as text, because the
adopted upstream vector turns on exactly that distinction. The
upstream test at tests/identity.py#fid.sign signs

    signed_message.encode("utf-8")

where signed_message is a 950-character hex string. The signed message
is therefore the ASCII text of that hex, 950 bytes, not the 475 bytes
it spells.
